Markets are pricing in real hike risk after Fed Chair Warsha's Jackson Hole speech lifted the two-year yield more than six basis points. The Bank of Canada faces its own Wednesday decision under renewed U.S. trade war pressure. Remember, we covered the central bank rate cycle yesterday.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:25.500
Today's 55% hike odds and 10-year yield move above 4.76% move that arc forward by raising the bar for any rate sensitive entry until after the September 15th to 16th FOMC meeting.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:34.580
Position sizing ahead of known macro events like Fed meetings requires shrinking exposure when the outcome is binary and volatility is elevated.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:49.480
Today's 55% probability of a 0.25% hike creates exactly that setup. The market has already moved the two-year yield higher, so any surprise in either direction will amplify moves in rate sensitive sectors.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:02:01.480
One practical implementation is to reduce position size by half on any name whose beta to the 10-year yield exceeds 1.2. Then re-enter only after the FOMC statement.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:13.180
This approach worked well in the 2022-2023 tightening cycle when pre-meeting cuts of 30-40% in size preserved capital on both hawkish and dovish surprises.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:24.840
The risk is missing a strong directional move if the outcome aligns with your base case, so the rule is to keep the reduced size for exactly one session after the decision before reassessing.

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:37.840
Worship's first Jackson Hole speech reset the market's rate cut math in one session. Every labor report between now and the September 15th to 16th meeting is now read against this new baseline.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:49.820
Bessence narrower rationale for debt management changes, slowing disorderly momentum rather than targeting equilibrium prices, further signals that the policy backdrop remains fluid.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:56.100
Now, here's something that most retail investors get wrong, and it cost me money before I figured it out.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:08.020
Imagine you added $5,000 to your TFSA last week into a single Canadian equity ETF right before the TSX dropped 1.5% on trade war headlines.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:19.160
Your order filled at the prevailing price but the real mechanics between click and fill involve how that ETFs underlying holdings reprise across sectors with different correlations.

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:33.820
Modern portfolio theory shows that combining assets whose returns do not move in lockstep reduces overall portfolio volatility without necessarily lowering expected return, pushing the portfolio toward the efficient frontier.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:43.600
Strategic allocation sets long-term weights by risk tolerance, while tactical allocation allows small deviations when valuations or macro signals shift.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:58.060
Canadian investors can implement this with all-in-one ETFs such as XEQT for 100% equity growth or VGRO for a 20% bond buffer, the historically dampened drawdowns.

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:07.040
Bond still serve a role post-2022 because their correlation with equities drops during equity sell-offs, even if yields are higher than before.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:20.680
Rebalancing on a threshold basis, for example, when any asset class drifts more than 5% from target, outperforms calendar rebalancing by reducing unnecessary trading costs while keeping risk in check.

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:33.480
The 10-year note yield crossing above 4.76% this week illustrates why even a modest bond allocation can act as a stabilizer when equity sectors rotate on central bank signals.

